{{also|Positron|positrón|pósitron}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|positive|tron}} ({{l|en|electron}}). {{coin|en|Carl Anderson|w=Carl David Anderson|nat=American|occ=physicist|in=1932}} to replace the earlier term {{m|en|antielectron}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɒz.ɪ.tɹɒn/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɑ.zɪ.tɹɑn/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-positron.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|particle}} The [[antimatter]] equivalent of an [[electron]], having the same [[mass]] but a [[positive]] [[charge]].
#: ''The notion of a '''positron''' weapon remains the stuff of science fiction.''
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=15 March 1933|work=Physical Review|volume=43|brackets=on|title=The Positive Electron|author=Carl D. Anderson|passage=If these particles carry unit positive charge the curvatures and ionizations produced require the mass to be less than twenty times the electron mass. These particles will be called '''positrons'''.}}

